{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "MosaicCoagulationEntryState",
    "description": "How MOSAIC turns one size bin of its column state into what coagulation integrates: SUBROUTINE mosaic_coag_1clm of chem/module_mosaic_coag.F (WRF v4.8.0), the per-bin block before the call to coagsolv (:155-293). Bins are independent here, so a consumer maps this over the eight sections; the outputs are the inputs of coagulation_assembly.esm (number concentration, one composition quantity per column, dry diameter) and of coagulation_kernel.esm (wet diameter and density).\n\nTHE STATE. MOSAIC carries, per bin, the number mixing ratio n (particles per mol of air), the ten component mixing ratios r_l (mol per mol of air), aerosol water and the hysteresis water (mol per mol of air), and three diagnostics kept alongside: the dry mass M = sum_l r_l mw_l, the dry volume V = sum_l r_l mw_l / rho_l (per mol of air) and the dry density rho = M/V.\n\nWHAT HAPPENS, in order. (1) The diagnostics are trusted unless they are implausible: if rho is outside [0.1, 20] g/cm3 or M differs from V rho by more than 1e-4 relative, M and V are recomputed from the composition (each r_l clipped at zero) and rho = M/V. (2) A bin whose dry mass is at most 1.01e-30 g/mol is treated as empty: default density 2 g/cm3, the number put back on the section's centre volume, water and hysteresis water dropped, the section's centre diameter used for both sizes. (3) Otherwise V is re-derived as M/rho and the number is CONFORMED into the section: a mean dry volume per particle below the section's lower bound (n > V/(f v_lo)) raises it to f v_lo and one above the upper bound (n < V f/v_hi) lowers it to v_hi/f, with the safety factor f = 1.00001 keeping it strictly inside. (4) The dry diameter is the sphere of the mean dry volume per particle, held at or above the section's lower diameter; the wet diameter is the sphere of the dry volume plus the water's volume, capped at 30 times the upper diameter; the wet density is (M + water mass)/(V + water volume). (5) coagsolv's composition columns are the ten r_l clipped at zero, then the hysteresis water, the water and the dry volume V, and its number is n times the molar air density.\n\nBRANCH COVERAGE AND REFERENCE. Over the 16 dumped chem170 steps none of the branches in (1)-(3) fires (7426 bin-level cells checked against the recorded entry state, every one on the plain path), so the eight cases taken from the capture pin only the plain path. Seven further cases drive the branches with SYNTHETIC inputs: one section at one level of a captured step is edited (EqWeFiC tools/perturb_mosaic_coag_entry.py) -- carried density above and below the plausibility bounds, a mass inconsistent with volume times density, an implausible density together with a negative component, an empty section that still carries both waters, and a number above and below what the section admits -- and the edited column is replayed through WRF's own mosaic_coag_1clm. Every case, captured or synthetic, is referenced against that REAL64 replay (kernels/mosaic_subproc_driver with the coag entry hooks, fork 66a1b222, -fdefault-real-8; data/eqwefic/chem170_scm/coag_entry64) at rel 1e-9; the real32 in-model record agrees with the replay to 4.7e-7. Mutation-checked: dropping the zero clip in the recompute fails 5 assertions, the empty-bin reset 8, the number conforming 6, the empty-bin water drop 1, the recompute itself 16. One choice the data still cannot distinguish: re-deriving the dry volume as M/rho on the plain path versus keeping the carried V, since a plausible triple has V = M/rho to 1e-4 and no edited case leaves the triple plausible but inconsistent below that bound.",
    "authors": [
      "EqWeFiC"
    ],
    "created": "2026-11-12T00:00:00Z",
    "tags": [
      "mosaic",
      "aerosol",
      "coagulation",
      "sectional",
      "wrf-chem",
      "chem_opt-170"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Zaveri, R. A., Easter, R. C., Fast, J. D. and Peters, L. K. (2008). Model for Simulating Aerosol Interactions and Chemistry (MOSAIC). J. Geophys. Res. 113, D13204."
      }
    ]
  },
